# Untitled

## Supramolecular Chiral Nanoarchitectonics
Exploration of molecular functions and material properties based on the control of chirality would be a scientifically elegant approach. Here, the fabrication and function of chiral-featured materials from both chiral and achiral components using a supramolecular nanoarchitectonics concept are discussed. The contents are classified in to three topics: i) chiral nanoarchitectonics of rather general molecular assemblies; ii) chiral nanoarchitectonics of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs); iii) chiral nanoarchitectonics in liquid crystals. MOF structures are based on nanoscopically well-defined coordinations, while mesoscopic orientations of liquid-crystalline phases are often flexibly altered. Discussion on the effects and features in these representative materials systems with totally different natures reveals the universal importance of supramolecular chiral nanoarchitectonics. Amplification of chiral molecular information from molecules to materials-level structures and the creation of chirality from achiral components upon temporal statistic fluctuations are universal, regardless of the nature of the assemblies. These features are thus surely advantageous characteristics for a wide range of applications.
## 1. Introduction
For various social demands including materials production,[1] environmental problems,[2] energy management,[3] and biological/biomedical treatments,[4] the most important key process would be the synthesis of functional materials. Recent developments in materials science demonstrates a high capability to create materials with various functions on the basis
of well-considered materials designs.[5] Small structural differences in component molecules are often capable of producing materials with totally different properties through organic syntheses,[6] supramolecular chemistry,[7] and materials processing.[8] Therefore, molecular designs for materials production can be regarded as the most important issue in science and technology in current society.
Amongst the many possibilities in molecular design for materials production, exploration of molecular functions and material properties on the basis of chirality controls would be a scientifically elegant approach. With identical elemental compositions and functional groups, but different configurations of these identical groups, functions such as chiroptical properties and chiral separations can be created.[9] Another important fact for chiral controls in molecular and materials science is the unavoidable deep relation
ship of chiral substances with biological activities.[10] Biomolecules are basically made of chiral components such as amino acids and saccharides. Therefore, molecular designs for biomedical drugs and bioactive materials must be produced upon critical control of their chirality. Due to the scientifically elegant approaches and biological importance, molecular sciences and materials technologies incorporating chirality continuously attract attention of scientists ranging from the fundamentals of the chirality of molecules and materials to bio-related applications. These research efforts spread into a wide range of scientific fields including basic physical chemistry,[11] chirality-controlled catalysis and synthesis,[12] chiral materials,[13] analyses of chiral structures,[14] chiral recognition,[15] chiral separation,[16] chiral plasmons and optics,[17] chiral drugs,[18] and biomedical related chiral sciences.[19]
Among the broad interests of chirality research, the recent hot trends of chirality related sciences are probably studies on chirality-based supramolecular chemistry and materials science upon the self-assembly of chiral molecular components[20] and achiral building blocks.[21] Enhancement of chiral effects and the creation of chirality are sometimes results of supramolecular organizations with chiral molecular units and even with achiral components. These systems are regarded as elegant examples of the emerging concept of nanoarchitectonics (Figure 1),[22] because control of simple molecular configurations can lead to the creation of such a wide variety of functional materials. The nanoarchitectonics concept was originally proposed by Aono and co-workers[23] as methodology to create functional materials from nanosized components through the combined
efforts of nanotechnology and other research fields including organic synthesis, supramolecular chemistry, materials science, and biology.[24] This concept requires the harmonization of several effects including atom/molecule manipulations, chemical procedures, self-assembly/self-organization, and nanostructure controls by external stimuli, and by including uncontrollable factors such as various perturbations, fluctuations, probability, and uncertainty[25] (Figure 1A). It can be applicable to a wide range of research fields including the production of nanostructured materials,[26] fabrication of nanostructures,[27] catalysts,[28] sensors,[29] environmental remediation,[30] energy-related technology,[31] devices,[32] and biological/biomedical applications.[33] Some of these examples include unavoidable contributions of chiral components and chiral structures.[9c,34]
Based on this background, herein, we focus on the fabrication and functions of chiral-featured materials from chiral and achiral components and utilize the supramolecular nanoarchitectonics concept. In order to exemplify the effect of the self-assembly nature of building blocks on features of the assembled materials, the contents are basically classified into three topics: i) chiral nanoarchitectonics of rather general molecular assemblies; ii) chiral nanoarchitectonics of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs); iii) chiral nanoarchitectonics in liquid crystals. In the initial topics, several examples are selected from supramolecular
assemblies and provide general images on chirality controls by molecular design and the control of chiral structures by external factors, and supramolecular chirality with achiral units. In the following sections, applications of chiral nanoarchitectonics to two representative materials, MOFs and liquid crystals are discussed.
In addition to general examples, MOFs and liquid crystals are especially highlighted herein because of their differences in the precision of interactions and flexibility of the assemblies. MOF materials are formed on the basis of precise metal–ligand interactions where directions of coordination are well-defined. MOF structures are based on nanoscopically well-defined coordination[35] (Figure 1B). Therefore, molecular chirality would be strictly transferred to the MOF materials. In contrast, liquid-crystalline materials have a partially fluidic nature and thus molecular chirality would have its effect with certain flexibility (Figure 1C). Mesoscopic orientation of liquid-crystalline phases are often flexibly influenced by local molecular structures.[36] Here, these materials systems with different structural flexibilities are highlighted. Discussions on effects and features in these representative materials systems with totally different natures would reveal the universal importance of supramolecular chiral nanoarchitectonics in supramolecular chemistry and materials science.
## 2. Chiral Nanoarchitectonics of Molecular Assemblies
Exploration of functional materials and structures with chiral molecular designs have been extensively researched. Although chiral molecular structures themselves have their intrinsic functions such as chiroptical properties, materials nanoarchitected from chiral molecular designs often show advanced functions. Therefore, fabrications for functional materials with chiral molecular units have been paid much attention. In this section, several examples of chiral functional materials through molecular assemblies are introduced.
## 2.1. Control by Molecular Design
Formation of extended assembled structures from chiral units would enhance chiroptical properties as materials. Nishihara
and co-workers fabricated 1D coordination polymers with a chiroptical bis(dipyrrinato)zinc(II) complex motif from a chiral (R or S) binaphthyl-bridged dipyrrin dimer ligand and zinc(II) acetate upon room-temperature mixing in a dichloromethane/methanol mixed solution.[37] The obtained materials can be exfoliated into single strands corresponding to a one directional coordination polymer with \(\approx 2600\) monomer units upon sonication of their solution. Interestingly, chiral photoluminescence of the formed 1D coordination polymers upon illumination with UV light at \(371\,\mathrm{nm}\) exhibited enhanced luminescence dissymmetry factor, which is 5.9 times greater than that for the corresponding monomers. Within the 1D coordination polymer chains, the steric stress of the units are not well released to enhance photoluminescence efficiency probably through deactivating the nonradiative decay.
Deng et al. reported the effect of chalcogen-substitution on the self-assembling behaviors of perylenediimide with chiral alkyl chains, \(N,N^{\prime}\)-bis((s)-(-)-3,7-dimethyloctyl)perylene-3,4:9,10-tetracarboxyldiimide (Figure 2).[38] Substitution of the bay region of the perylenediimide moiety by S and Se modulate van der Waals interactions to regulate supramolecular chirality of perylenediimide units in chloroform–methanol mixed solutions. Microscopic observations revealed that left-handed helical ribbons were formed upon substitution with S but that incorporation of Se resulted in right-handed line morphology. Based on calculations with density functional theory, it was suggested that steric hindrance of the chiral alkyl chains and chalcogen atoms affect rotation angles between neighboring molecules in their assembly to minimize total system energy. In addition, S–S interactions has great influence for assembling the motif but contribution of Se–Se interactions is weak. An n-type nature of perylene as an organic semiconductor often shows good sensing capability for electron donor guests such as ammonia. Incorporation
of S and Se and the resultant assembling structures significantly affect sensor sensitivities upon modulation of conductive path.
Olson and co-workers demonstrated chiral memory effects in dynamically formed self-assembled supramolecular helices (Figure 3).[39] Addition of a chiral \(\pi\)-amino acid donor, tryptophan, to an amphiphilic acceptor with \(\pi\)-electron-deficient bis-bipyridinium units resulted in helical nanoarchitectures with a sub-millimeter scale length and controlled handedness, reflecting the transcription of molecular chirality to microscopic morphologies. The formed helical structures have dynamic and robust natures. Exchange of donor components could be done but the helical structures were maintained during partner donor exchange. Based on these fundamental characteristics of the helical fibers, the chiral memory effects were realized as seen in the reservation of chiral handedness upon exchanges of chiral donors to achiral \(\pi\)-electron donors such as melatonin and di(ethylene glycol)-disubstituted 1,5-diaminonaphthalene. These processes enable us to nanoarchitect microscopic helical structures with desired handedness from totally achiral components. The demonstrated strategies on nano-to-macroscale chiral translation and its chiral memory effects upon acceptor–donor interaction would be useful for chirality amplification, chirality storages, and chirality transfer in various materials systems.
Not limited to typical chiral structures, chiral controls of microscopic structures on the basis of molecular chirality origins have been investigated with various morphologies. For example, Percec and co-workers showed rare examples of the formation of chiral columns from chiral supramolecular spheres.[40] Supramolecular spheres formed from conical and crown-shaped molecules are usually assembled into cubic and tetragonal phases, but transformation into columnar phase is unusual. They investigated the self-assembly of a chiral denronized cyclotetraveratylene derivative in which eight chiral first-generation minidendron are included, and unexpectedly found formation of a columnar phase from chiral supramolecular spheres (Figure 4A). Direct observations of circular dichroism spectroscopy revealed chirality preservation from the spheres to columns. Supramolecular column motifs are fundamental in periodic liquid-crystalline and arrayed crystalline phases, therefore featuring chiral natures to supramolecular columns provides some intuitions in columnar-phase-based materials nanoarchitectonics.
Vacogne et al. reported the transformation of \(\alpha\)-helical polypeptides into enantiomorphic microscopic spirals.[41] The microscopic spirals in their dried state (Figure 4B) were fabricated through direct emulsification of \(\gamma\)-benzyl-glutamate-based polypeptides in their homo-form and copolymers with allylglycine. The spiral senses are solely determined with chirality of polypeptide unit, glutamate. Its \(\alpha\)-helical right-handed polypeptides with L-glutamate residues resulted in the formation of clockwise spirals, while spirals architectures in counterclockwise motifs were obtained from D-isomer-dominated \(\alpha\)-helical left-handed polypeptides. Detailed and exact mechanisms of the transformation of molecular chirality into a microscopic spiral are hidden under veils, because formation of spiral structures may be based on hierarchic assembling processes. It can be proposed that transmission of chirality and helicity from molecular structures to microscopic structures may be driven by hierarchic processes of fibril bundling of coiled \(\alpha\)-helices.
As an interesting example of the regulation of biological phenomena by chirality-controlled structures, Feng and
co-workers found cooperative effects of molecular chirality and supramolecular chirality on adhesion behaviors of living cells.[42] They used gelator molecules having two phenylalanine derivatives at the end bridged with various spacers to form helical fibrils for hydrogel formation (Figure 4C). Within the hydrogel materials, helical fibers were formed and their handedness (right-handed and left-handed) was controlled by odd-even effect of the spacer methylene units. In addition, adhesion and proliferation properties of mouse embryonic fibroblast cell and human-derived EA.hy926 endothelial were also examined on 2D films of hydrogels. Cell adhesion capabilities were enhanced on left-handed nanofibers of L-phenylalanine derivatives, while right-handed nanofibers with L-phenylalanine derivatives provided only weak influence. Both the right-handed and left-handed nanofibers from D-phenylalanine derivatives resulted in negative and weak effects on cell adhesion, respectively. Mutual cooperation of molecular chirality and microscopic helicity would lead to new thinking for materials designs for biomedical applications.
Although single chiral molecules only display limited properties, nanoarchitected materials upon self-assembly of chiral unit molecules often show advanced functions including optical, electrical, and biological effects, as exemplified above. Small molecular chirality information can be transformed into microscopic asymmetric chiral structures, leading to new functions in various sizes. Therefore, coupling of chiral molecular designs with advanced modes and concepts including hierarchical assembly,[43] instructed assembly,[44] temporally controlled assembly,[45] self-assembly within \(1\mathrm{D}^{[46]}\) and 2D structural[47] motifs, and self-assembly with \(\pi\)-conjugation[48] would create novel properties and functions in future-expected developments.
## 2.2. Control by External Factors
Instead of using intrinsic chiral molecular information, the application of external factors to target materials and the alteration of conditions of systems can regulate chiral structures and related functions. For example, Choi et al. reported the delicate regulation of supramolecular chirality of mixing materials systems.[49] They demonstrated chiral transfer and inversion through changes of mixing ratio between chiral component (calix[4] arene derivatives with chiral alanine side chains) and achiral component (achiral bipyridine compounds) accompanied with the rolling up from 2D sheets to tubular structures. Upon mixing of chiral calix[4] arene compounds, the chirality can be transferred to an achiral bipyridine derivative, and inversion of the supramolecular chirality was induced by addition of 0.6 equivalent of the chiral components. In these processes, 2D sheets in hydrogels were formed initially upon mixing of 0.2 equivalent of chiral calixarene derivatives and increase of the mixing ratio up to 0.6 equivalent induced morphological transformation to rolled-up tubes. The observed chiral inversions and morphological transformations are basically driven through modulation of intermolecular hydrogen bonding between the chiral and the achiral components accompanying re-orientation in their assembled structures as suggested by density functional theory calculations. These strategies and
concepts would be effective for faint chirality control in biological and supramolecular systems with proximity of chiral and achiral molecules.
As shown in the previous examples, addition of interactive metal ions is an efficient method to modulate chiral structural information of molecules and supramolecular assemblies. Wang and Feng reported the regulation of supramolecular chirality of molecular gelators with two L-phenylalanine residues upon addition of metal cations, including controls of the twist pitch and the diameter of the chiral 1D structures (Figure 5).[50] The observed chiral structural modifications are driven by the synergic effects of intrinsically formed hydrogen bonding and regulated coordination with metal ions. These gelator molecules are essentially capable of assembling through hydrogen bonding at the amide and carboxylic acid moieties that can be influenced by coordination at carboxylic acid groups with added metal ions. For example, \(\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) exhibited stronger coordination capability to the carbonyl groups as compared with \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\), resulting in weakening of the hydrogen bonds. Synergetic effects of these interactions resulted in modulation of chiral structures including inversion of chiral twists. The observed chiral modulations with rich possibilities would be useful for various applications such as chiroptic, asymmetric catalysis, and sensing.
Precise chirality controls of molecular assemblies often becomes possible by shifting the assembling media from solution to surface. For example, Tahara et al. investigated determination factors on handedness of two-dimensionally assembled porous molecular network formed with alkoxysubstituted dehydrobenzo[12] annulene at the 1-phenyloctane/ graphite liquid-solid interface on the basis of scanning tunneling microscopic observations (Figure 6).[51] Interdigitate assemblies of chiral alkoxy chains between neighboring alkoxysubstituted dehydrobenzo[12] annulenes determine the relative disposition of adjacent molecules together with handedness of the porous network. Therefore, the handedness of the 2D supramolecular assembly is not decided only by the absolute configuration of the corresponding chiral center and is actually determined under influences of both the numbers of chiral centers and the length of the achiral parts of the alkoxy chains. These detailed observations revealed the unavoidable roles of the achiral structural parts on chiral structures of the supramolecular assembly.
De Feyter and co-workers proposed a novel method to create a chiral surface environment, nanocorrrals, by nanoshaving at a liquid-solid interface.[52] Chiral orientationally controlled nanocorrrals were fabricated by removing covalently bound aryl molecules from highly oriented pyrolytic graphite surface by the tip of a scanning tunneling microscope. Handedness of the nanocorrral can be controlled upon their orientation with respect to the underlying graphite surface, which provides
a chirality modulated surface for the adsorption of prochiral molecule 5-octadecyloxy-isophthalic acid to form a chiral 2D assembly. High freedom of shapes and orientation of nanocorrrals is advantageous for the fabrication of chiral 2D assemblies from various achiral molecules. Not limited to the formation of chiral structures, this methodology would be also useful for enantioselective adsorption of racemic substances.
Tang and co-workers demonstrated the fabrication of chiral photonic crystals by angle modified Langmuir–Schaefer method of \(\mathrm{NiMoO}_{4}\!\cdot\!x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) ultrathin nanowires (diameters less than \(2\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) on a surface of ethylene glycol (Figure 7).[53] The \(\mathrm{NiMoO}_{4}\!\cdot\!x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) nanowires floated on the subphase were assembled in a uniaxial alignment by slow compression and then the aligned nanowires were transferred on to a quartz plate by the Langmuir–Schaefer method. The series of layers were transferred with regularly shifted rotational angles of the solid substrate in a clockwise or counterclockwise manner to result in right or left-handed photonic crystals. This methodology provides precise interlayer structures with programmable manner and useful for the manipulation of various optically active thin films.
As shown in the abovementioned examples, chiral structural information of assembled macroscopic materials is not always decided by the molecular configuration of chiral centers. The relation between molecular chirality and materials chirality such as handedness of twisted structures is not always simple. Therefore, alteration of conditions and application of external factors can modulate materials chirality and resulting functions, which is an important essence in supramolecular chiral nanoarchitectonics.
## 2.3. Chirality Creation from an Achiral Unit
Chiral molecular configuration is not always a decisive factor for materials-level chirality as mentioned above. As seen in several examples in previous sections, chiral information can be transferred from chiral structures to achiral components. With these situations, one can expect that materials chirality can be created even from achiral components. In addition to pioneering contributions on supramolecular chirality from achiral building blocks by Liu and co-workers,[54] such chirality creation in materials and molecular systems from achiral sources have been paid attention in recent research activities.
Katoono et al. demonstrated chirality creation through the assembly of three achiral molecular rod units (1,4-bis(phenylethynyl)benzene) connected in macrocycle molecular frameworks, where the rods are further bridged by terephthalamide units (Figure 8).[55] Twisting of the two amide groups in each bridge with respect to the central benzene ring induces dynamic chiral forms in \( M \)- or \( P \)-helicity. In this molecular design, two types of chiral molecular systems, i.e., \( C_{2} \)-symmetry (MMP and PPM) or \( D_{3} \)-symmetry (MMM and PPP), becomes possible under interconvertible dynamic chirality conversion. Conformational chiral transition between chiral structures can be regulated upon complexation with chiral guests through hydrogen bond formation. For example, addition of chiral ditopic guest carrying two amino groups efficiently form hydrogen bonding with carbonyl groups of the achiral rod molecules, resulting in facile chiral conversions.
It is known that self-assembled structures of achiral components sometimes result in chirality-biased structures in positive
or negative directions as static happening. Hud and co-workers demonstrated symmetry breaking in the spontaneous formation of supramolecular polymer structures from achiral 2,4,6-triaminopyrimidine and a cyanuric acid derivative (Figure 9).[56] The assembled structures exhibited active circular dichroism signals although all the component molecules are achiral. Static average of intensity and signs of circular dichroism signals becomes zero when many trials were repeated. The mixed system causes reversible supramolecular polymer structures with temporal chirality from achiral components. Morphology
observation with scanning electron microscopy confirmed formation of twisted helical fibers with one handedness in one domain and opposite handedness in another domain. Addition of only a small amount of a chiral analogue leads to the formation of homochiral morphologies exclusively. Due to triaminopyrimidine derivatives being regarded as a mimic of nucleic acid bases, these phenomena may be related with the origin of nucleic acid homochirality.
Sánchez and co-workers investigated the formation of helical supramolecules induced by chain-dependent helix and helix interactions between polymers of 1,3,5-triphenylbenzenetricarboxamides derivatives with chiral and achiral side chains (Figure 10).[57] From achiral components, \(P\cdot\) and M-type atropisomer mixtures were formed due to restricted rotational freedom of the benzamide units. The formed helix structures assembled into further hierarchic motifs through helix–helix interactions. The helix–helix interaction was well promoted through interdigitation of aliphatic achiral side chains accompanied by self-sorting to attain preferable a heterochiral helix–helix supra-assembly. Formation of higher level assemblies sometimes preserve local asymmetric chirality of achiral components.
Formation of chiral twisted supramolecular assemblies has been demonstrated using various achiral components. Feng and co-workers reported twisted nanoribbons from a chiral \(\pi\) -conjugated amphiphilic oligo( \(\boldsymbol{p}\) -phenylenevinylene)
derivative, oligo( \(\boldsymbol{p}\) -phenylenevinylene)- \(\cdot b\) -poly(ethylene glycol). Twisted chiral nanotubes and nanoribbons with biased chirality were formed with these achiral components in well-tuned solvent systems.[58] Formation of chiral structures would be driven by directional \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking accompanied by the steric repulsion of poly(ethylene glycol) chains. At the initial stage, the chiral structures are originated under statistical fluctuations, which can be extended to an accidental excess of handedness in higher-level of structures such as the ribbons and the fibers. Similarly, formation of twisted chiral nanoribbons from achiral conjugated oligoaniline derivatives was reported by Wei and co-workers.[59] In their case, chiral-bias can be regulated by changing solvent systems (increase of iso-propanol content in iso-propanol/water mixed solution). Furthermore, chiral separation of racemic phenylalanine was demonstrated using the fabricated single-handed twisted nanoribbons. Bhosale and co-workers reported the formation of supramolecular assemblies from achiral naphthalene diimide with urea interacting groups.[60] The assembling behaviors including handedness controls were regulated by mixing ratios of solvent systems between tetrahydrofuran and methylcyclohexane. Optically inactive achiral naphthalene diimide molecules in tetrahydrofuran can be converted to optically active left-handed helical aggregates in a tetrahydrofuran and methylcyclohexane \((70/30\%\) and \(60/40\%\) , \(\upnu/\upnu\) ratio) solvent mixtures.
Steed and co-workers investigated the formation of supramolecular helix from achiral tris(urea) and pentakis(urea) peptidomimetic compound and their chiral amplification.[61] Adjacent helices aggregate into fibrils that intertwine to form high-fidelity braids with periodic crossing patterns. The chirality of these assemblies was amplified according to their innovative method. Hot precursor sol was filtered through cotton wool, affording right-handed fibrils. Cotton has intrinsic chiral structures predominantly consisting of cellulose with D-glucose residues, and then chiral amplification of sensitive assemblies of origo(urea) compounds can be achieved.
An air–water interface is a medium to amplify molecular interactions[62] and appropriate for molecular conformational tuning.[63] In fact, conformational adjusting and helicity inversion of chiral binaphthyl residues were successfully demonstrated.[64] As another example of chirality control at the air–water interface, Bai and co-workers investigated chiral reorganization at the air–water interface using achiral double-decker phthalocyanine (Figure 11).[65] The achiral double-decker cerium phthalocyanine were spread on a water surface and transferred onto a solid substrate by Langmuir–Schaefer method. The transferred films are circular dichroism active although the film components are achiral with uneven distributions of circular dichroism signals. These results may suggest local symmetry breaking at interfacial media.
As seen in the abovementioned examples, creations of chirality in supramolecular assemblies from achiral building blocks are quite possible under statistical fluctuations. The initially created unbalanced chiral distributions can be amplified with external stimuli and procedures. Contacting target materials with the other chiral materials and fixing systems at heterogeneous interfacial environments are also effective ways for the chiral amplification.
## 3. Chiral Nanoarchitectonics of Nanoporous Materials: MOFs
In the above sections, various features of chirality controls in supramolecular assemblies are discussed using rather uncategorized examples. In the following sections, materials for discussion are focused on two kinds of materials, MOFs and liquid crystals. MOF materials are nanoarchitected with local coordination interactions with submolecular-level precision of positions and orientations of interacting moieties[66] while molecular assembling motifs in liquid crystals are rather flexible and soft.[67] Unlike other nanoporous materials with continuous material bodies such as mesoporous silica, mesoporous carbon, and the others, MOF materials are supramolecular assemblies from organic ligands, which fit well with the focus here.
## 3.1. Fabrication of Chiral MOFs
Chirality is also an attractive concept in the science and technology of MOFs from the viewpoint of both structural fabrications and functional applications.[68] Nanoarchitectonics approaches for chiral MOF materials are roughly classified into two strategies: i) use of chiral organic ligands as building blocks and ii) chiral induction from chiral templates. As one
of the pioneering examples of chiral MOF materials and their asymmetric functions, Kim and co-workers demonstrated the fabrication of chiral MOFs and their usage for enantioselective catalysis (Figure 12).[69] They used chiral organic building blocks synthesized from chiral tartaric acid derivatives for chiral MOFs. In the formed MOF structures, pyridyl groups are exposed to the MOF open channels and work as catalytic sites for transesterification. Reaction rates showed significant dependence on bulkiness of the substituent of the reactant because of size-regulated channel structures of the MOF. More interestingly, these catalytic sites immobilized within the chiral MOF structures exhibited enantiomeric excess in the reaction products.
Biological components are typically chiral and the use of biomaterials as components for chiral MOFs would be a logical method. Sawada et al. demonstrated the preparation of MOF-like nanoporous structures from short peptides upon metal coordination (Figure 13).[70] The tripeptide ligands, glycine–L-proline–L-proline (Gly–Pro–Pro), were assembled with silver ions to make crystalline coordination materials with folded peptide helices, polyproline II helix. The fabricated coordination materials also have helical nanochannels with large diameter (more than \(2~\mathrm{nm}\) ). The chiral nanochannels exhibited enantioselective guest uptakes of anions, organic molecules, and biological substances. For example, racemic 1,1'-bi-2-naphthol molecules can be resolved by simple soaking of the chiral MOF crystals to the guest solution. The formed channels are large enough to accommodate biomolecules, maltopentaose, a linear pentasaccharide with five D-glucose residues can be encapsulated. The structure and properties may be regarded as mimics of protein structures such as active pockets of enzymes. They may be good candidates for artificial materials with bio-related functions such as artificial enzymes and artificial receptors.
Synthetic efforts for chiral MOF materials using chiral organic linkers are not always easy because extensive efforts to choose proper conditions are sometimes required. As an alternate strategy, Kim and co-workers demonstrated postsynthetic modification to introduce chiral ligands onto pre-synthesized stable achiral MOF. The host achiral MOF structures possess thermally and chemically stable frameworks with large pores and open metal coordination sites.[71] As postsynthesis, chiral ligands, [(S)-N-(pyridin-3-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide] and [(S)-N-(pyridin-4-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide], were attached at the open coordination sites of the host achiral MOF structures to introduce chiral L-proline residues. The synthesized chiral MOF materials exhibited remarkable catalytic performances in asymmetric aldol reactions between various aromatic aldehydes and ketones with moderate enantiomeric selectivity.
Introduction of chiral amino acid and oligopeptide residues into MOF structures with the aid of microwave irradiation was reported by Canivet and co-workers.[72] The host MOFs were formed from 2-aminoterephthalates and indium octahedra as building blocks. The amino groups immobilized within the host MOF structures work as grafting points for attachment of amino acids and oligopeptides. Upon use of well selected coupling reagents, N-protected amino acids and N-protected oligo peptides with the aid of microwave irradiation, followed
by deprotection of the \(N\)-terminals. The microwave irradiation promotes the coupling reactions within pores of the host MOFs with reasonable yields under mild conditions. These mild coupling conditions are also beneficial for suppression of racemization of amino acid and oligopeptide residues. The attachment of various species from single amino acid to tetrapeptides were experimentally demonstrated. Coupling reactions between amino groups and carboxylic acids are common in organic chemistry, especially with chiral biomolecules, therefore this strategy would become a widely applicable method for the introduction of chiral molecular units into MOF. The prepared materials would find application opportunities in chiral sensing, chromatographic chiral separation, and enantioselective catalysis.
Instead of using chiral building blocks, the transfer of chiral structural information from surrounding molecules, templates, and additives is also an effective method to prepare chiral MOFs. For example, Morris and co-workers demonstrated chiral induction from chiral ionic liquids as solvents for the ionothermal synthesis of MOF materials.[73] The MOF, composed of octahedrally coordinated nickel bound to four trimesate bridges, was synthesized in the chiral ionic liquid 1-butyl 3-methylimidazolium L-aspartate. Although the building blocks themselves are achiral, the MOF has a homochiral structures.
Ionic liquids are significantly structured due to their strong Coulombic interactions. Therefore, it is highly expected that chiral ionic liquids possess asymmetric long-range distribution of their chiral structural information. Preparation of MOF under an asymmetric environment successfully introduced chirality to intrinsically achiral MOF motifs. Crystallographic analyses on chiral MOF crystals revealed strong binding of ionic liquid cations inside of the MOF pores, suggesting significant interaction between the MOF and solvent molecules.
Chiral induction can be achieved in the presence of small amounts of chiral additives instead of using chiral solvents.
Bu and co-workers successfully prepared homochiral MOF crystals from achiral components, thiophene-2,5-dicarboxylic acid with \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\), through the addition of small amounts of chiral additives, \((-)\)-cinchonidine or \((+)\)-cinchonidine.[74] Solvothermal reactions of thiophene-2,5-dicarboxylic acid and \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\) in dimethylformamide or diethylformamide with or without cinchonidine provided several types of MOF crystals with the same coordination modes, where each \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) site binds to four thiophene-2,5-dicarboxylic acid ligands and each carboxylate group chelates to only one \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) site. A conglomerate mixture was obtained without the use of any chiral additives. Homochiral MOF crystals were yielded upon addition of \((-)\)-cinchonidine as a cosolute additive, while the handedness of the MOF crystals prepared with \((+)\)-cinchonidine became opposite. Chiral crystallization of MOF materials was completely regulated by the presence of a small amount of chiral additives.
Inclusion of chiral guests into nanochannels of achiral MOF structures sometimes induces chirality creation as demonstrated by Zaworotko and co-workers.[75] MOF materials prepared with a 6-connected tetranuclear \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\) cluster and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate linkers are highly symmetrical achiral structures. Chiral versions of this symmetrical MOF structure can be fabricated in the presence of L-proline or D-proline like the other chiral MOF systems. Interestingly, incorporation of achiral guest molecules of \(N\)-methyl-2-pyrrolidone to an achiral MOF structure can also induce chirality creation. Inclusion of the guest may result in distortion of symmetrical MOF structures, resulting in a chiral MOF as a racemic conglomerate.
Inclusion of guest molecules in MOF channels can be used for visualization of enantiomeric excess upon circular dichroism imaging as recently reported by Yamada et al.[76] They synthesized a chiral MOF using an amino acid–lanthanum complex as a chiral precursor and a trimesic acid as a bridge building block. With induced circular dichroism upon inclusion of achiral dye, methyl orange, into the prepared chiral MOF, enantiomeric excess of the chiral MOF was quantitatively estimated using 2D circular dichroism mapping with high spatial resolution close to the diffraction-limit. Evaluation of enantiomeric excess in microparticle size regime can become possible, actually revealing that high enantiomeric excess was achieved at a microparticle level.
These examples indicate that the preparation of a chiral MOF can be accomplished through the use of chiral building blocks, use of chiral template (solvents or additives), and chiral guest inclusion. These efforts are based on molecular level precise consideration of coordination design. The obtained chiral MOF structures are crystalline in many cases, therefore relation between molecular designs and the resulting materials structures can be discussed with precise analyses. The concept of the direct extension from supramolecular design to materials structures is well applicable in the case of chiral MOF preparation.
## 3.2. Application of Chiral MOFs
As mentioned above, chiral MOFs are able to keep precise chiral molecular information in their practical uses. Therefore, several chirality-based applications are expected. Enantioselective separation based on chiral recognition is one of
important application. Although many chiral MOF materials have chiral recognition capability, precise mechanisms on chiral recognition within MOF channels are not always clear. Kim and co-workers presented pioneering work on the analyses of interactions between a chiral MOF and chiral guest molecules using in situ single-crystal an X-ray diffraction method.[77] The single crystals of a host–guest complex between the homochiral MOF \([Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(1)\)-benzenedicarboxylate) \((\mathrm{L}(-)\)-lactate) \((N,N^{\prime}\)-dimethylformamide)] and chiral 1-phenyl-1-ethanol guest were subjected to single-crystal X-ray diffraction using synchrotron radiation. The analyzed results indicate that hydrogen bonding \(\mathrm{O}^{3.4}\mathrm{H}^{3.4}\mathrm{C}\) between the chiral guest and pendant \(N,N^{\prime}\)-dimethylformamide ligand has a crucial role in chiral recognition. For the framework structure of the chiral MOF, \(N,N^{\prime}\)-dimethylformamide does not have any meaningful contributions, but has an important role in guest recognition. Such unexpected mechanisms can become clear only with precise analyses by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. Detailed structural analyses are important for the understanding of mechanisms of chiral recognition in MOF structures.
The crystalline sponge method as reported in pioneering work by Inokuma et al., single-crystal X-ray analyses on guest-doped MOF crystals provided structural information of non-crystallized guest molecules.[78] Yaghi and co-workers reported the use of single-crystal X-ray diffraction techniques for chiral structure determination upon selective accommodation of chiral guests into channels of a chiral MOF.[79] Fixing of a guest molecule at a defined site within regularly aligned MOF pores highly suppresses motional freedoms of the anchored guest molecule and helps ordered alignments of the trapped guest molecules. In addition, absolute chiral structures of the host MOFs can work as a reference for determination of absolute chirality of the trapped guest molecules. Availability of this method for determination of absolute chiral configuration was demonstrated for a wide range of guest molecules including complex plant hormones.
Zhang et al. reported combined functions of a crystalline sponge for determination of an absolute chiral configuration and a chiral stationary phase for gas chromatography using one kind of chiral MOF material.[80] The used chiral MOF material composed of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(4,4^{\prime}\)-bipyridine bridges with chiral mandelate was synthesized as nanocrystals with dimensions of \(\approx500\ \mathrm{nm}\). This MOF motif has several advantageous features for target applications including homochirality, high hydrolytic and thermal stability, tight binding site for effective chiral recognition, and highly crystalline nature and low-symmetry space group. The latter characteristics are crucial for the determination of absolute chiral configurations without using a reference standard. For in situ chiral separation, nanocrystals of the chiral MOF materials were coated at inside walls of a capillary column with thickness of \(\approx0.7\ \mathrm{mm}\). The enantiomeric species with longer retention time in column separation is subjected to chiral crystalline sponge analyses, enabling both enantiomeric purification and chiral identification.
As mentioned in several examples in the previous section, catalyst use for enantioselective reactions is one of the promising applications of chiral MOF materials. In addition to precise chiral channel structures for substrate accommodation, organization of catalytic sites provides advantageous
benefits. Liu and co-workers demonstrated heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis using chiral MOF materials (Figure 14),[81] which conversions and enantioselectivity corresponded to those for homogeneous catalytic systems. The applied catalysts adopt (R)-6,6′-dichloro-2,2′-dihydroxy-1,1′-binaphthyl-4,4′-bipyridine as an axially chiral bridging ligand for a homochiral MOF structure. Easily accessible chiral dihydroxy groups in the chiral MOF can immobilize \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{OiPr})_{2}\) to provide a Lewis acidic catalytic site. Enantioselective catalysis for the production of chiral secondary alcohols through the addition of \(\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}\) to aromatic aldehydes. The immobilization of catalytic sites within MOF structures can realize highly dense (same as high concentration) and ideally uniform distributions of catalytic sites, therefore MOF-based catalysts would have much higher performances (conversion and enantiomeric selectivity) than conventional heterogeneous solid catalysts and may be comparable with homogeneous catalysts.
Based on a self-assembling nature, MOF-based catalysts have fine tunability of the catalytic cavity structure. Lin and co-workers nanoarchitected a series of chiral isoreticular MOFs as tunable hosts for asymmetric catalysis using various tetracarboxylic acids with orthogonal chiral dihydroxy functional groups in the (R)-enantiomeric motif.[82] The chiral dihydroxy groups can react with \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{OiPr})_{4}\) to give Lewis acidic sites,
which can catalyze addition of diethylzinc to aromatic aldehydes to result in chiral secondary alcohols. The synthesized chiral MOF catalysts was sufficiently stable under the reaction conditions and provided the corresponding chiral secondary alcohols with various enantiomeric excess values depending on the pore geometries of the MOF. Dependence of enantiomeric excesses of the reactions on the open channel sizes is caused by different diffusion rates of reaction substrate molecules in MOF channels, which alters the balance between the pore-catalyzed enantioselective reaction and the background non-selective reaction.
Commement of catalytic sites within chiral MOF channels results in efficient cooperative catalytic reactions with high enantiomeric selectivity. Cui and co-workers synthesized chiral MOF materials based on VO(salen) complex (Figure 15).[83] The VO(salen) complexes are known to catalyze cyanation of aldehydes to produce cyanohydrins. Especially bimetallic catalysis with two VO(salen) complexes are capable of faster reactions and higher enantiomeric selectivity as compared with a monometallic catalysis mechanism. The synthesized VO(salen)-based catalytic chiral MOF showed significantly high enantioselectively (up to \({>}99\%\) enantiomeric excess) for the cyanation of aldehydes. Detailed analysis on crystal structures of the chiral MOF indicated that twisted VO(salen) units are uniformly lined with open V sites exposed to the open MOF channels. The distance between adjacent \(\mathrm{V}{-}\mathrm{V}\) in face-to-face orientation is ideal for intermolecular cyanide transfer. Therefore, a bimetallic route becomes much more favorable, resulting in significant enhancement of the reaction rate and enantioselectivity.
They also synthesized multivariate MOFs with different metallosalen organic bridges. These metallosalen units of different atoms in MOF structures become adjacent to each other through interpenetration of the MOF structures.[84] Cooperative effects between different catalytic sites under these structural situations resulted in improved reaction efficiencies and enantioselectivity in asymmetric sequential alkene epoxidation/ epoxide ring-opening reactions, which can exceed the summation of the individual catalysts.
Distinct advantages in biocatalytic systems would be the fact that reaction cascades are rationally and systematically achieved without changing conditions or the addition of external compounds. Nanoarchitecting auto-tandem-type reactors would be a way to mimic excellent bio-catalytic systems. Incorporation of multiple catalytic sites within one MOF structure can lead to the fabrication of auto-tandem catalysts. Han, Qi, and co-corkers successfully prepared chiral MOF materials with multiple functional sites including Keggin-type \(\mathrm{[ZnW_{12}O_{40}]^{6-}}\) anions, \(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\) , \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -bipyridine as bridging unit, and pyrrolidine-2-yl-imidazole as asymmetric organocatalyst, to catalyze \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) fixation to chiral cyclic carbonates.[85] Tandem reactions including asymmetric \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) fixation and the activation of epoxidation intermediates were working together in a single MOF. This cascade reaction proceeded in a highly enantioselective manner.
Chiral MOF strategies enables us to scale up chiral structural information with molecular-level precision to microscopic and macroscopic levels. Therefore, the use of chiral MOFs would be one of the most promising methodologies to exploit molecular chirality at a materials level. The porous nature of the materials structures allows other types of applications such
as chiral-sensitive sensing and drug delivery, which would be anticipated in addition to chiral separation and chiral catalysis described here.
## 4. Chiral Nanoarchitectonics of Mesoscopic Materials: Liquid Crystals
Unlike MOF materials with rather fixed rigid structures containing molecular-level structural precisions, liquid-crystalline materials have soft natures with rather flexible molecular interactions. Therefore, discussions on chirality and related functions for these contrasting materials are meaningful. Next to the previous MOF specific sections, the following sections describe chirality related sciences and functions with liquid-crystalline materials. Liquid crystals have the capability of forming soft materials with flexibly tunable chiral properties.
## 4.1. Chirality in Liquid Crystals
The flexible nature of assembled structures of liquid-crystalline materials often create a variety of material states, such as polymorphism. Hegmann and co-workers demonstrated polymorphism of liquid crystals of a bent-core-type molecule that can be controlled solely by cooling rate (Figure 16).[86] The used bent-core molecules have an achiral chain (1-octyloxy chain on the para-side) and a chiral chain ((R)-2-octyloxy chain) at the other side of the bent-core (tris-biphenyl diester core). Assembly of this chiral molecule provided thermally stable liquid crystals in oblique columnar phase, while kinetically favorable helical microfilament phase was formed upon rapid cooling. Control experiments using an analogue molecule with two achiral chains and another molecule with two chiral chains suggested that a thermodynamically formed phase can be more favorably formed with achiral chains and the presence of achiral chains in the bent-core molecule tend to form a kinetically favorable phase. In addition to cooling rate, molecular designs including chirality and symmetry would have unavoidable contributions on the selection of phases.
Chiral nematic (cholesteric) liquid crystals with broadband reflection capabilities are highly potential materials with many possible usages such as polarizer-free displays, switchable reflective windows, optical data storage, broadband polarizers, and continuous waveband laser protection. In fabrication of liquid-crystalline materials for broadband reflection, control of pitch gradients in chiral nematic-liquid-crystal materials is important. Yang and co-workers synthesized an isosorbide derivative with thiyl groups at both terminals because isosorbide derivatives are known as a fine chiral dopant with a high helical twisting power and high miscibility with host liquid crystals.[87] So-called thiol-acrylate chemistry using photopolymerizable isosorbide derivatives with thiyl groups and conventional liquid-crystalline acrylate monomers are used for polymer-stabilized chiral nematic-liquid-crystalline films for tunable broadband reflection. Systematic investigations on fabrication procedures with influential factors finally realized the successful preparation of films with a reflection bandwidth of over \(2000\;\mathrm{nm}\).
Because liquid crystals are soft materials, addition of small stimuli and additives sometimes induces changes of the entire materials structures. Chiral induction can be done with addition of small amounts of chiral molecules. Unlike these conventional approaches, Hegmann and co-workers demonstrated highly
efficient chiral induction by addition of cholesterol-carrying gold nanorods to nematic liquid crystal (4-cyano-4'-pentylbiphenyl) (Figure 17).[88] Gold nanorods with an aspect ratio of 2.2 or 4.3 were capped with chiral cholesterol molecules through siloxane linkages. Effect of the gold nanorods with cholesterol is much more efficient than usual chiral dopants. Only with one-order lower chiral concentration the cholesterol-decorated gold nanorods can have comparable effects with much tighter helical distortion in the host liquid crystals. Alignment of the shape-asymmetric nanorods along the distorsional direction of the liquid crystals was confirmed. The observed effective chiral amplification may be originated in remote (not only molecular interaction) effects with elastically coupled feedback.
Addition of colloidal particles with particular shapes to liquid crystals may disturb the long-range interactions. Effect of particle topology, especially chirality of colloidal particles, is of interest. Smalyukh and co-workers investigated the addition of artificially fabricated chiral colloidal particles in the forms of right-handed and left-handed springs and helices synthesized by two-photon-absorption-based polymerization, to nematic liquid crystals.[89] With particle tracking using laser tweezers and their optical imaging, influences of distortions upon addition of the chiral springs and helices on self-organized structures in nematic liquid crystals were investigated. Chirality of
colloidal particles and elasticity of nematic liquid crystals would determine colloidal superstructures in chiral or racemic states. The observed phenomena were also simulated by numerical modelling on the basis of Landau–de Gennes free energy.
Structural control of supramolecular assemblies depends significantly on the design of components. From this viewpoint, DNA origami pieces synthesized under pre-programmable designs would be ideal building blocks for self-assembly,[90] as seen in recent examples for regulated supramolecular assemblies of DNA origami building blocks.[91] Dogic and co-workers used programmed DNA origami filaments to form chiral colloidal liquid crystals.[92] They synthesized DNA origami filaments with pre-programmed microscopic twist and aspect ratios. Above a certain concentration range, solutions of these filament DNA species form stable chiral nematic liquid crystals. Helical pitch and chirality handedness can be controlled by molecular designs of DNA origami filaments and their mixing. Mixing of right-handed and left-handed DNA origami filaments provided a chiral nematic phase in one chirality but dependence of pitches of the chiral nematic phase on mixing compositions is not linear unlike the other conventional system. In addition, fabrication of DNA origami filaments into 1D twisted ribbons and 2D colloidal membranes were also demonstrated.
This research bridges DNA origami technology with a high programmable capability of unit structure and chirality-controlled materials and colloidal science, which would have important contributions in DNA-based nanoarchitectonics.[93]
In soft and flexible liquid-crystalline materials, factors to induce chirality in entire materials are not limited in the range of directly interacting vicinities. Addition of a small amount of molecules and nanomaterials with chiral structural information can induce changes to macroscopic morphologies of liquid crystals.
## 4.2. Chirality Switching and Control
The soft and flexible nature of liquid crystals allows dynamic modulations of their functions including chirality switch and control as exemplified below. As advanced strategies to bridge molecular motions to material structures, regulation of materials systems including liquid crystals by molecular machines is an attractive target. Molecular machines are known as molecules and supramolecules with machine-like motions such as rotation upon application of external stimuli.[94] Motions of molecular machines have been investigated traditionally in solution[95] and recently at a solid surface.[96] In addition, working behaviors of molecular machines in soft materials and interfacial media including liquid surfaces[97] and cell membranes[98] have been paid attention. Chiral regulation of supramolecular rotors with clockwise and counterclockwise rotation upon applied biases were reported by Uchihashi and co-workers.[99] Recently, the chiral switching of a liquid-crystalline phase can be triggered by actions of molecular motors as demonstrated by Brasselot and co-workers (Figure 18).[100] They prepared chiral nematic phases by addition of a chiral binaphthyl molecule and light-driven molecular motors to liquid crystals. Control of light irradiation can cause chirality inversion where rotation of defect structures caused by light irradiation can be visually observed. Spontaneous symmetry breaking in directionality rotation might be caused by interplay between supramolecular rotation and diffusion of rotation centers under non-equilibrium conditions.
Li and co-workers successfully fabricated a liquid-crystal transparent display on the basis of molecular switches with light-driven chiral fluorescence capabilities (2,5-bis(α-cyanostyryl) thiophene motif or 1,4-bis(α-cyanostyryl) benzene motif with two binaphthyl side chains (Figure 19).[101] They synthesized novel chiral fluorescent switch molecules with reversible \(Z/E\) photoisomerization properties together with a tunable fluorescence intensity. The synthesized molecular switches were added to achiral nematic host liquid crystals to form chiral nematic liquid crystals with reversible reflection color tuning over wide spectrum range from the visible to infrared regions. Transparent with dual mode display film devices were fabricated with these chiral liquid-crystalline materials. Modulation of chirality strength leads to the formation of chiral nematic films that are operable either exclusively in fluorescent mode or in both fluorescent and reflection mode. The well-designed films can function under daylight and darkness with fast response and high resolution, demonstrating both a useful device and remote-writing board.
Selective light reflection can be made through the regulation of helical superstructures of chiral nematic liquid crystals, but variation ranges are limited in most cases. Yu and co-workers reported precise tuning of chiral structures of liquid-crystalline materials in a wide range using a newly synthesized molecule.[102] The synthesized molecule has two different photoswitch motifs (azobenzene and o-fluoroozobenzene) and one chiral structure (binaphthalene) which can work as a tristable chiral switch. Basic isomerization caused by irradiation at 365 and \(470\ \mathrm{nm}\) light resulted in a (trans, trans, trans, trans)-configuration and (cis, cis, cis, cis)-configuration, respectively. Irradiation with another light at \(530\ \mathrm{nm}\) induced the trans–cis isomerization of the o-fluoroozobenzene part and caused cis–trans transformation of the azobenzene, totally resulting in a (cis, trans, trans, cis)-configuration. These three stable configurations lead to continuous and adjacent tuning of the reflection from chiral nematic liquid crystals. The system is capable of covering the entire visible spectrum ranges as well as near-infrared region. The proposed piece-wise tuning in cholesteric liquid crystals would become a good candidate for phototunable red, green, and blue (RGB) colors and a black reflective display.
Phase changes between a chiral nematic phase and untwisted lamellar chiral smectic A phase of liquid crystals were demonstrated using a hydrazone-based light switch with light-sensitive dopants as reported by Aprahamian and co-workers (Figure 20).[103] The synthesized light switch molecule has an isosorbide scaffold with two 4-decyloxyphenyl benzoate moieties as a bistable light switch part, which was used as a dopant in nematic liquid crystals. The doped liquid
crystals exhibited light-dependent bistability between the chiral phase and untwisted lamellar phase through kinetic trapping of various assemblies. Transition to a chiral nematic phase upon light irradiation at \(340\;\mathrm{nm}\) increased the transmittance minimum to \(920\;\mathrm{nm}\). Chiral switching to an untwisted lamellar chiral smectic. A phase upon irradiation at \(410\;\mathrm{nm}\) made the thin film highly transparent. The reported controls of transparency upon chiral modulation of the liquid-crystalline films can be used for optical windows with light-gated functions.
As demonstrated in various supramolecular systems, accumulation of molecular motions can be converted to macroscopic materials motions. Tamaoki and co-workers demonstrated rotation of macroscopic object upon accumulation of the molecular crankshaft effect (Figure 21).[104] Cyclic chiral azobenzene-based motors were doped in nematic-liquid-crystalline hosts. \(E/Z\) photoisomerization of the azobenzene motor molecules can be reversibly accomplished accompanied by large shifts in helical twisting power. An appropriate sequence of light irradiations induced continuous conversions of back and forth structural changes of the chiral azobenzene motor molecules, resulting in rotation of macroscopic glass flakes on the chiral nematic-liquid-crystalline film. The molecular crankshaft effect can
convert piston-like motions to rotational motions that can be a mimic of engines in macroscopic world.
Control of chiral molecules in liquid crystals can switch the chiral structures of liquid crystals at a macroscopic level. Such changes are often expanded to submicron-size with the creation of various optical functions. Further size expansion can lead to controls of visible-sized objects.
Formation of chiral supramolecular structures through the assembly of achiral components has been paid much attention. In addition, such chiral symmetry breaking in soft materials including the fluidic phase is often accompanied by orientational order of component molecules. Pociecha and coworkers fabricated heliconical smectic C phases from achiral molecules, 4-[[4-([6-[4-(4-cyanophenyl)phenyl]hexyl]oxy)phenyl] methylidene]amino]phenyl-4-alkoxy-benzoates.[105] These molecules adopted a twisted structural motif in both nematic and lamellar phases including formation of a heliconical tilted smectic C phase and a tight pitch heliconical nematic phase. In the various chiral structures, gradual freezing of molecular rotation was confirmed. The reported bent dimer molecules strongly tend to form twisted structures. This molecular twisting led to formation of hierarchic helical structures, which transmit into even micrometer level within soft flexible structures.
Anisotropic fabrication of achiral liquid-crystalline molecules can induce photo-stimulated chiral motions. Katsonis and co-workers fabricated patterned enantiomerically paired soft springs with photo-switchable molecules for macroscopic chiral actuators (Figure 22).[106] A set of bars with low and high liquid-crystalline order states were synthesized through photo-polymerization of polymerizable azobenzene-based
photoswitch molecules in monomeric nematic crystals. Every set of bars exhibited transformation upon light illumination with different shape changes. Accumulation of these anisotropic motions led to large photomechanical responses, finally resulting in formation of two chiral macroscopic helices. These chiral motion systems were made only by fabrication efforts without using any chiral molecular sources.
Naota and co-workers demonstrated chirality transfer from solvent (chiral nematic liquid crystals) to achiral solute molecules (trans-bis(salicylaldiminato)nickel(II) complexes) to form supramolecular chiral fibers.[107] The used achiral coordination complexes usually form achiral supramolecular fibers in achiral nematic-liquid-crystalline phases and non-mesogenic crystals. In contrast, helicity intensity and direction of the supramolecular gel fibers can be controlled in chiral nematic liquid crystals as solvents depending on helical twisting nature and chiral dopant concentrations. One important key to control helicity of the gel fibers is flexibility of the herringbone-based lamellar alignment of the trans-bis(salicylaldiminato)nickel(II) complexes, as confirmed by X-ray diffraction analyses.
## 4.3. Chiral Liquid-Crystal Materials: Cellulose Nanocrystals
As mentioned above, research approaches on chiral liquid crystals are not limited to organic chemistry and supramolecular
chemistry and actually have strong features of materials science. Therefore, the viewpoint of materials science becomes important in chiral liquid crystals. As an emerging category of chiral materials, liquid crystals based on cellulose nanocrystals have been paid attention.[108] In the following section, recent research examples on chiral liquid crystals of cellulose nanocrystals are briefly explained.
Precise modulation of the pitch of liquid-crystalline materials in a range of visible light wavelengths would lead to photonic materials operable in the entire range of the visible spectrum. Zhou and co-workers fabricated composite films from chiral nematic cellulose nanocrystals and poly(ethylene glycol) to provide humidity-responsive structural colors (Figure 23).[109] Structures of cellulose nanocrystals were optimized with synthetic conditions, where control of charge density is an important factor for pitch control. The pitch of composite films in chiral nematic structures were tuned through changing of mixing ratio with poly(ethylene glycol), giving tuning of structural colors from blue to red. The composite films prepared from chiral cellulose crystals and poly(ethylene glycol) have excellent mechanical and thermal stability with high uniformity as well as low cost nature in their fabrication process. It will become a useful material in various applications including inks, decorative coatings, and colorimetric sensors.
MacLachlan and co-workers utilized chiral liquid crystals of cellulose nanocrystals to synthesize pressure-responsive chiral photonic aerogel materials with the aid of an ice-templating technique.[110] Growth of ice crystals in chiral liquid crystals of cellulose nanocrystals affected helical pitches of the chiral liquid crystals through squeezing the liquid crystals. Sublimation of ice from the mixture resulted in macroporous structures
as low-density aerogel materials. Hierarchic structures were formed in the aerosol where cellulose nanocrystal walls took a 2D nematic phase and were further assembled into ribbons as supports for a 3D cellular network. The porous aerogels have sufficient flexibility, meaning the 3D structures can be transformed upon pressure-induced rearrangement. Although the porous aerogels themselves exhibited a white color, incorporation of solvent led to bright iridescent colors depending on solvents. This new type of mechano-responsive photonic materials would be used as solvent-responsive inks.
They also developed stimuli-responsive stretchable optic materials using a reversible unwinding phenomena of chiral nematic liquid crystals of cellulose nanocrystals embedded in stretchable elastomers (Figure 24).[111] The composites were fabricated upon first filling the monomer mixture (ethylacrylate and 2-hydroxyethyl acrylate) into a piece of cellulose nanocrystal film followed by polymerization. The fabricated composite films of chiral nematic cellulose nanocrystals and elastomer are stretchable with large deformation possible by applying mechanical stress, and removal of the stress reverted the film to the original state. The film stretching induced the appearance of interference colors upon unwinding of the chiral nematic liquid crystals, and color disappearance was observed upon reversal of the mechanical transformation between chiral nematic and pseudo-nematic phases. These materials have possibilities to be used as sensing materials capable of visualization of local mechanical deformation to detect structural failures.
Sun et al. demonstrated the use of cellulose crystals as templates to transfer chiral information to chiral materials.[112] In their approach, flexible ternary composites from latex, graphene oxide, and cellulose nanocrystals were synthesized by evaporation-induced self-assembly. Uneven distribution of the components can be realized using common stimuli such as gravity. In the next step, cellulose nanocrystals were selectively etched by alkali treatment with successful chirality transfer to achiral materials. The resulting composite films retain chiral nematic order and asymmetric distribution of graphene oxide, which is sensitive for swelling toward solvents. Color changes and actuation behaviors were achieved using various mixture between water and alcohols.
As demonstrated above, the use of cellulose nanocrystals as chiral liquid crystals creates various functional materials on the basis of their chirality and pitch of twisted structures. Celluloses are naturally abundant biomass materials, meaning cellulose nanocrystals may become key materials for practical applications of chirality-based science and technology.
## 5. Brief Summary and Perspective
Herein, we have briefly summarized several features of materials fabrications with chirality-featured structures and functions through supramolecular assembly of chiral components and/or achiral building blocks. Chirality is only a tiny piece of molecular information mostly derived from a configuration around a single carbon atom, but it is capable of regulating chirality, helicity, and organization of microscopic and macroscopic materials upon supramolecular assembly. The feature can be observed commonly in general supramolecular assemblies, bond-specified precise assemblies (MOFs), and flexible and soft assemblies (liquid crystals). This amplification of chiral molecular information to materials-level structures is universal regardless of the nature of assemblies and is thus surely an advantageous characteristic for a wide range of applications. Therefore, various chirality-based applications including optical regulations, sensors, separations, and biomedical issues have been continuously paid attention in future technologies.
Chirality control is not only based on the decisive transfer of chiral structural information from molecules to materials. In some cases, materials with asymmetric chiral structural information and functions can be created from achiral building blocks. Such chiral creation and symmetry breaking can be driven by the application of asymmetric external stimuli. In addition, chiral structures can be also be created through self-assembly from achiral components upon temporal and/or statistic fluctuations. The latter process can be regarded as the use of asymmetry within fluctuation in production of realistic functional materials. Currently, it has been paid attention as a method of symmetry breaking in basic science. However, it possesses a high potential as a new type of materials science including practical applications. If we would wisely establish methodology to utilize temporal and statistic fluctuations for the production of functional materials, we would not always have to synthesize chiral building blocks with complicated asymmetric organic synthesis. Without decisive chemical structures, we may be able to create asymmetric high-functionality systems through
the amplification and fixation of temporal fluctuation and statistic uneven distributions. This strategy fits well with the nanoarchitectonics concept that takes fluctuation and inhomogeneity into account for materials production through self-assembly and self-organization (see Figure 1, again). The nanoarchitectonics concept would have a crucial contributions in future developments of chiral-based materials science and technology.
